same way. Read both.


1. THE CODE

   The Rust, HTML, CSS, build scripts and configuration — and the comments
   inside them, which are part of the code and may be copied with it.

   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (see LICENSE-APACHE), with
   one added condition:

     ATTRIBUTION. Any work that uses this code, in whole or in part, must
     display the contents of the NOTICE file somewhere its own users can see
     it — an about screen, a colophon, a footer, a credits page, or the
     equivalent for whatever it is. Keeping the notice only in the source is
     not enough.

   Everything else in Apache-2.0 applies unchanged, including the patent
   grant and the disclaimer of warranty.


2. THE PROSE

   The English sentences this site displays: the page text, the taglines, the
   questions and the answers to them. Whether a sentence sits in a Markdown
   file or inside a Rust string literal makes no difference to this.

   Not licensed. Copyright is reserved.

   Quote it as copyright law allows anybody to quote anything. Do not
   reproduce it as the text of another site.

   Take the machinery and write your own words. The machinery is the part
   that was hard.


A NOTE ON WHAT THIS IS

Because of the added condition, and because the prose is not licensed at all,
this repository as a whole is not open source under the OSI definition. The
code is open with a condition attached; the writing is not open. Said plainly
here so that nobody has to work it out from the files.
